= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $T=\dot\gamma$ along an affinely parametrized <geodesic> and $D_t=\nabla_T$ for the <covariant derivative> from the <Levi-Civita connection>. We use the <Riemann curvature tensor> convention $R(X,Y)Z=\nabla_X\nabla_YZ-\nabla_Y\nabla_XZ-\nabla_{[X,Y]}Z$, for which positive <sectional curvature> means $\langle R(X,Y)Y,X\rangle>0$ on an orthonormal pair. A <Jacobi field> is a smooth <vector field> along $\gamma$ satisfying
$$
\boxed{D_t^2J+R(J,T)T=0.}
$$
Such a field describes the first-order separation of nearby <geodesics>, as the <geodesic variation> construction in part (c) shows.

For $t_0>0$, $\gamma(t_0)$ is a <conjugate point> of $\gamma(0)$ along this <geodesic> if a nonzero <Jacobi field> satisfies $J(0)=J(t_0)=0$. The <multiplicity of a conjugate point> is
$$
\boxed{\dim\{J:D_t^2J+R(J,T)T=0,\ J(0)=J(t_0)=0\}.}
$$
It is equivalently the dimension of the <kernel> of the <differential> of the <Riemannian exponential map> at $t_0\dot\gamma(0)$, by <Jacobi fields vanishing at their initial point>. Conjugacy concerns the specified <geodesic> and its two parameter values, even when its endpoints represent the same point of the <Riemannian manifold>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Suppose $J$ is a <Jacobi field> vanishing at both endpoints of a nonconstant <geodesic> segment $[0,t_0]$. Its tangential component $h(t)=\langle J(t),T(t)\rangle$ satisfies
$$
h''(t)=\langle D_t^2J,T\rangle=-\langle R(J,T)T,T\rangle=0,
$$
because $D_tT=0$ and the <Riemann curvature tensor> has the usual skew-adjoint symmetry. The two zero endpoint values imply $h\equiv0$. Both $J$ and $D_tJ$ are therefore perpendicular to $T$.

The <linear map> from these <Jacobi fields> to $T(0)^\perp$ given by $J\mapsto D_tJ(0)$ is injective: $J(0)=0$ together with $D_tJ(0)=0$ forces $J\equiv0$ by uniqueness for the linear <ordinary differential equation> defining a <Jacobi field>. Its target has dimension $n-1$, so
$$
\boxed{\text{multiplicity}\leq n-1.}
$$
This proves the <endpoint-vanishing normal Jacobi fields have dimension at most n-1> bound. For a constant <geodesic> the equation is $J''=0$, and two zero values instead force $J=0$, so there is no <conjugate point>.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $p=\gamma(0)$, $v=\dot\gamma(0)$, $a=J(0)$ and $b=D_tJ(0)$. Choose the short curve $c(s)=\exp_p(sa)$ and let $P_s:T_pM\to T_{c(s)}M$ be <parallel transport> along $c$. The tangent vectors
$$
V(s)=P_s(v+sb)
$$
satisfy $V(0)=v$ and $\nabla_sV(0)=b$. Define a <geodesic variation> by
$$
\boxed{F(s,t)=\exp_{c(s)}\bigl(tV(s)\bigr).}
$$
For each fixed $s$ this is an affinely parametrized <geodesic> with initial point $c(s)$ and initial velocity $V(s)$. Although the <Riemannian manifold> need not be complete, smooth dependence for the <geodesic equation> on a neighborhood of the compact reference segment provides a single $\varepsilon>0$ on which $F$ exists for all $|s|<\varepsilon$ and $0\leq t\leq1$. Thus completeness is unnecessary.

Set $X(t)=\partial_sF(0,t)$. The torsion-free <Levi-Civita connection> gives $\nabla_s\partial_tF=\nabla_t\partial_sF$. Differentiating $\nabla_t\partial_tF=0$ with respect to $s$, and commuting the two <covariant derivatives>, yields
$$
D_t^2X+R(X,T)T=0.
$$
Also $X(0)=c'(0)=a$ and $D_tX(0)=\nabla_sV(0)=b$. Uniqueness for the <Jacobi field> equation now gives **$X(t)=J(t)$** on the whole segment. This is <realization of Jacobi fields by geodesic variations>.

The printed formula switches the order of its two variables. The consistent notation here is $F(0,t)=\gamma(t)$ and $J(t)=\partial_sF(0,t)$. A parametrized surface here means a smooth variation map; it cannot be required to be an <immersion>, since the allowed <Jacobi field> $J=0$ would already violate that condition along the reference curve.

