= Solution
{parent=a}

Consider the subset
$$
A=\{x\in M:f_1(x)=f_2(x),\ (df_1)_x=(df_2)_x\}.
$$
It is nonempty by the given initial data. A <local isometry> preserves the <Levi-Civita connection> and therefore affinely parametrized <geodesics>. On a sufficiently small <normal neighborhood> of $x\in A$, this gives
$$
f_i(\exp_xv)=\exp_{f_i(x)}((df_i)_xv).
$$
The right sides agree, so $f_1=f_2$ on that neighborhood and their <differentials> agree there as well. Consequently $A$ is <open>.

It is also <closed>. If $x_j\in A$ converges to $x$, continuity first gives $f_1(x)=f_2(x)$. Choose coordinate charts around $x$ and this common image. The coordinate matrices of the two <differentials> depend continuously on the base point; their equalities at $x_j$ therefore pass to $x$. Thus $x\in A$. Since $M$ is <connected>, the nonempty subset $A$ that is both open and closed equals $M$. Hence **$f_1=f_2$ everywhere**. This proves <local isometries are determined by first-order data> without requiring completeness.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $\pi:\widetilde M\to M$ be the <universal cover> and equip it with the pullback <Riemannian metric> $\widetilde g=\pi^*g$. The <covering map> is a <local isometry>, so $\widetilde M$ has constant <sectional curvature> one.

We must check completeness of the lifted <Riemannian metric>. A <geodesic> on $\widetilde M$ projects to a <geodesic> on $M$. By <geodesic completeness> of $M$, the projected <geodesic> extends for all real time. Its unique lift with a specified initial point extends the original lifted <geodesic>, because $\pi$ is a <local isometry>. Hence $\widetilde M$ is geodesically complete, and the <Hopf-Rinow theorem> also gives metric completeness.

The allowed constant-curvature form of <Cartan-Ambrose-Hicks theorem> says that a linear tangent-space <isometry> between complete simply connected <Riemannian manifolds> of the same constant <sectional curvature> extends to a global <isometry>. The unit round <sphere> $S^n$ is complete, simply connected for $n\geq2$, and has <sectional curvature> one. Comparing it with $\widetilde M$ therefore yields
$$
\boxed{(\widetilde M,\widetilde g)\cong S^n.}
$$
This is the covering step in the <classification of complete positive constant-curvature manifolds>. As usual for this assertion, $n\geq2$ is understood: in dimension one there are no sectional two-planes, and the <universal cover> of the circle is the line, not the circle.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

By part (b), $M=S^n/\Gamma$, where the group of <deck transformations> acts freely by round <isometries>. Every round <isometry> is the restriction of an <orthogonal transformation> of $\mathbb R^{n+1}$: it preserves the inner product $\langle x,y\rangle=\cos d(x,y)$, so its images of an orthonormal basis determine its linear extension.

Suppose $n$ is even. Every $A\in SO(n+1)$ has eigenvalue one. Indeed, the nonreal <eigenvalues> come in conjugate pairs with product one, while the remaining real eigenvalues are $\pm1$. Their number is odd and their product is one, forcing at least one $+1$. Such a transformation fixes a unit vector. Freeness of the <deck transformation> action consequently implies that the <determinant> map $\Gamma\to\{\pm1\}$ has trivial kernel. Thus $|\Gamma|\leq2$.

If $\Gamma$ is nontrivial with generator $A$, then $A^2=I$. Its eigenvalues are $\pm1$, and freeness excludes the eigenvalue $+1$. Hence $A=-I$, the antipodal <involution>. We obtain
$$
\boxed{M\cong S^n\quad\text{or}\quad M\cong\mathbb{RP}^n.}
$$
This proves the even-dimensional part of the <classification of complete positive constant-curvature manifolds>.

**The conclusion fails in odd dimensions.** For $n=2m-1\geq3$, identify $S^{2m-1}$ with the unit sphere in $\mathbb C^m$. Scalar multiplication by $\zeta=e^{2\pi i/r}$, with $r\geq3$, generates a freely acting <cyclic group> of <isometries>: $\zeta^jz=z$ for nonzero $z$ forces $\zeta^j=1$. The quotient is complete with constant <sectional curvature> one and has <fundamental group> $C_r$, so it is neither the simply connected <sphere> nor <real projective space> with fundamental group $C_2$. In particular $S^3/C_3$ is a <lens space> counterexample.

= 3
{parent=Paper 16}
